{
  "about": "The three forms a `sub` can have in v2 and how each is spelled (ADR 0020, \"The claims of a token\"; ADR 0019, \"Assigning a subject\"; ADR 0022, \"SPIFFE identities\"; REQ-SUBJ-071, REQ-WORKLOAD-008). shape: Classify says person (the person's own subject, 128 random bits as 26 lower-case base32 characters, the sub of a tenant whose sector is public), pairwise (a Mac cut to 256 bits as 43 base64url characters, ADR 0034, which ADR 0045 cancels and the code still makes) or spiffe (the SPIFFE ID of a workload), and an empty kind for a string that is none of them: an e-mail address, a name of the upstream, a v1 spelling, a person's subject whose spare bits are set. ci_job and service_account: the SPIFFE ID made from what a verified proof says, or the part that was refused (trust_domain, segment); length (an ID of more than 2048 bytes) is tested in the unit test, as the vector would be as long as the limit. The ID names the repository or the ServiceAccount and never the run. v1_spellings: what v1 wrote as `sub` for each kind of subject, with the v2 form where v2 has one (v2 is null where v2 issues nothing like it); v2 never issues a v1 spelling, so each v1 string classifies as none, and a v2 given for a v1 CI or ServiceAccount spelling is the ID that the inputs make. decision says where the change is recorded: ADR 0015 (cutover), ADR 0022, or open (a question to the maintainer, on issue 1816). kits/go/subject is held to this file; session and exchange are held to shape for the sub of a person.",
  "limits": {
    "trust_domain_max": 255,
    "id_max_bytes": 2048,
    "trust_domain_alphabet": "a-z 0-9 . - _",
    "segment_alphabet": "A-Z a-z 0-9 . - _, not empty, not . or .."
  },
